Suppose a firm can perform a task with a worker or with a machine. Let $\gamma(x)$ be the productivity of an hour of labor at task $x$ relative to that of a machine-hour, and let $p_m$ be the rental of a machine-hour. Labor holds the task when $w\le p_m\gamma(x)$, and at an interior margin $x^*$, where the firm is indifferent,
\optdisp{w=p_m\gamma(x^*).}{}
We call $p_m\gamma(x)$ labor's replacement value at task $x$. This factor-price condition is the one used at the task margin by Acemoglu and Restrepo (2018), with the same threshold logic in Zeira (1998) and, for offshoring, in Grossman and Rossi-Hansberg (2008). We keep the condition and abstract from the force that restores labor in those models, the creation of new labor tasks, a modeling choice motivated by our reading of history and recent U.S. measures. Tasks closed to machines, by capability or by rule, form a wall, and a worker employed only there earns a wage determined within the human-required sector. This paper examines that margin with two questions.

\textbf{What prices the substitute?} A machine is itself produced, from other machines, labor, energy, sites, minerals, permissions. Its rental belongs to the same cost system as the goods and services households consume. Tracing input costs through production resolves the rental of a machine-hour into its price coefficients $\widetilde\lambda_m$ and $\widetilde b_m$, the human hours and non-produced services of its full supply chain:
\optdisp{p_m=w\widetilde\lambda_m+r\widetilde b_m,}{}
where $r$ is the rent on non-produced inputs, resources whose supply is not generated by the production process. Land and mineral deposits are examples, and a grid connection or fabrication bottleneck can occupy the same position over a horizon on which its capacity is fixed. With the task margin, $w=p_m\gamma(x^*)$, this gives
\optdisp{\frac{w}{r}=\frac{\gamma(x^*)\widetilde b_m}{1-\gamma(x^*)\widetilde\lambda_m}.}{}
Holding the other terms fixed, the wage--rent ratio falls when machines improve at the marginal task (a lower $\gamma(x^*)$, task automation) and when the machine chain needs less labor (a lower $\widetilde\lambda_m$, recursive automation). The denominator is the feedback from the wage into the cost of its substitute. When no labor remains in the machine chain, $w/r=\gamma(x^*)\widetilde b_m$, and in the flow benchmark the machine's price is accounted for by its claim on the non-produced input.

\textbf{What prices the alternative to working at all?} Life outside employment still requires somewhere to live, food, energy, and care. Let a household or public institution provide each person one basket of these, at cost $P_s$, whether or not the person works, and let the wage buy further baskets. A person whose market hour has utility cost $\chi>0$ then works exactly when
\optdisp{w\ge s,\qquad s=(e^{\chi}-1)P_s,}{}
so the reservation wage rises with the cost of the basket. The production network prices the basket as it prices the machine. In the flow benchmark $P_s=wL_s+rB_s$, where $L_s$ and $B_s$ are the total human hours and non-produced services one basket requires. Since $P_s\ge rB_s$, the wage measured in baskets is at most $(w/r)/B_s$, and since every basket needs living space, $B_s\ge h>0$, the baskets an hour buys, and with them the gain from work, tend to zero as the wage--rent ratio tends to zero.

This paper endogenizes the two sides of the labor margin. The two chains end at the same prices:
\optdisp{\begin{array}{c@{\hspace{2.5em}}c}\textit{labor demand} & \textit{labor supply}\\\Big\downarrow\rlap{$\scriptstyle\ \gamma(x^*)$} & \Big\downarrow\rlap{$\scriptstyle\ \chi$}\\\textit{replacement cost} & \textit{outside option}\\[2pt]\llap{$\scriptstyle\widetilde\lambda_m,\,\widetilde b_m\ $}\searrow\qquad & \qquad\swarrow\rlap{$\scriptstyle\ L_s,\,B_s$}\\[2pt]\multicolumn{2}{c}{\textit{rents on non-produced inputs}}\end{array}}{}
Task automation acts on the first link of the demand chain, through relative human productivity at the marginal task, $\gamma(x^*)$. Recursive automation acts on its second link, through the labor embodied in machine services, $\widetilde\lambda_m$. The supply chain passes through the cost of the household's living basket, which the same cost system prices through the hours $L_s$ and non-produced services $B_s$ the basket requires, and through the cost of work $\chi$. The firm compares a worker with a machine, and the person compares a wage with support, so the wage is measured against the rents on non-produced inputs from both sides of the market. Participation depends on wage purchasing power, the support available outside employment, and preferences. Technological change also moves the prices of the inputs on which production and consumption depend.

This paper endogenizes the two sides of the labor margin. On the demand side, labor demand leads to the replacement cost of labor, and the replacement cost to rents on non-produced inputs. On the supply side, labor supply leads to the outside option, and the outside option to the same rents. One cost system prices the machine substitute and the household's living basket. Participation depends on wage purchasing power, the support available outside employment, and preferences. Technological change affects relative capability at tasks (``task automation''), the labor embodied in machine services (``recursive automation''), and the prices of the inputs on which production and consumption depend. The two chains compare the wage with other prices, and the labor market fixes its level. The non-produced input is in fixed supply, so it limits how many baskets the economy produces and with them how many hours it employs. Hours required must equal the hours people choose to supply,
\optdisp{\frac{TL_s}{B_s}=NF\left(\log\left[1+\frac{w}{P_s}\right]\right),}{}
where $T$ is the endowment of the non-produced input, $N$ the number of potential workers and $F$ the distribution of the cost of work. Task assignment sets the requirements $L_s$ and $B_s$, so this condition and the replacement price together determine the marginal task, the wage--rent ratio, and participation. Under explicit funding conditions the equilibrium exists and is unique (Theorem 1).
